WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.036 --> 00:01.546 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference May 1,

00:01.546 --> 00:03.276 align:middle
2024 CHAIR POWELL.

00:03.276 --> 00:03.876 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:04.206 --> 00:06.426 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:06.426 --> 00:09.586 align:middle
on our dual mandate to
promote maximum employment

00:09.676 --> 00:11.616 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

00:11.956 --> 00:15.516 align:middle
The economy has made
considerable progress toward our

00:15.516 --> 00:16.786 align:middle
dual-mandate objectives.

00:17.776 --> 00:19.506 align:middle
Inflation has eased
substantially

00:19.506 --> 00:21.916 align:middle
over the past year, while
the labor market has

00:21.916 --> 00:22.716 align:middle
remained strong.

00:23.566 --> 00:24.746 align:middle
And that's very good news.

00:25.456 --> 00:29.616 align:middle
But inflation is still too high,
further progress in bringing it

00:29.616 --> 00:33.126 align:middle
down is not assured, and the
path forward is uncertain.

00:34.586 --> 00:36.526 align:middle
We are fully committed
to returning inflation

00:36.526 --> 00:37.816 align:middle
to our 2 percent goal.

00:39.076 --> 00:40.936 align:middle
Restoring price stability
is essential

00:40.936 --> 00:44.296 align:middle
to achieve a sustainably strong
labor market that benefits all.

00:46.126 --> 00:49.306 align:middle
Today, the FOMC decided to
leave our policy interest rate

00:49.306 --> 00:52.966 align:middle
unchanged and to continue to
reduce our securities holdings,

00:53.066 --> 00:54.296 align:middle
though at a slower pace.

00:56.686 --> 00:57.626 align:middle
Our restrictive stance

00:57.626 --> 01:00.006 align:middle
of monetary policy has been
putting downward pressure

01:00.006 --> 01:03.406 align:middle
on economic activity and
inflation, and the risks

01:03.406 --> 01:04.766 align:middle
to achieving our employment

01:05.006 --> 01:07.746 align:middle
and inflation goals have
moved toward better balance

01:07.836 --> 01:08.756 align:middle
over the past year.

01:10.246 --> 01:13.116 align:middle
However, in recent months,
inflation has shown a lack

01:13.116 --> 01:15.266 align:middle
of further progress toward
our 2 percent objective,

01:16.036 --> 01:18.486 align:middle
and we remain highly
attentive to inflation risks.

01:19.716 --> 01:21.586 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about monetary policy

01:21.586 --> 01:24.106 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:25.966 --> 01:29.036 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest that
economic activity has continued

01:29.036 --> 01:30.966 align:middle
to expand at a solid pace.

01:31.846 --> 01:34.956 align:middle
Although GDP growth
moderated from 3.4 percent

01:35.016 --> 01:37.746 align:middle
in the fourth quarter of
last year to 1.6 percent

01:37.806 --> 01:41.206 align:middle
in the first quarter, private
domestic final purchases-which

01:41.206 --> 01:43.926 align:middle
excludes inventory
investment, government spending

01:43.926 --> 01:47.166 align:middle
and net exports, and usually
sends a clearer signal

01:47.166 --> 01:50.576 align:middle
on underlying demand-was 3.1
percent in the first quarter,

01:51.206 --> 01:53.746 align:middle
as strong as the
second half of 2023.

01:54.196 --> 01:57.886 align:middle
Consumer spending
has been robust

01:57.886 --> 01:59.416 align:middle
over the past several quarters,

01:59.836 --> 02:02.346 align:middle
even as high interest rates
have weighed on housing

02:02.406 --> 02:03.506 align:middle
and equipment investment.

02:04.846 --> 02:07.846 align:middle
Improving supply conditions
have supported resilient demand

02:08.266 --> 02:09.276 align:middle
and the strong performance

02:09.276 --> 02:11.706 align:middle
of the U.S. economy
over the past year.

02:11.996 --> 02:17.006 align:middle
The labor market remains
relatively tight, but supply

02:17.006 --> 02:19.356 align:middle
and demand conditions have
come into better balance.

02:20.586 --> 02:24.546 align:middle
Payroll job gains averaged
276,000 jobs per month

02:24.546 --> 02:28.056 align:middle
in the first quarter, while the
unemployment rate remains low

02:28.056 --> 02:29.576 align:middle
at 3.8 percent.

02:30.666 --> 02:33.516 align:middle
Strong job creation over the
past year has been accompanied

02:33.516 --> 02:37.296 align:middle
by an increase in the supply of
workers, reflecting increases

02:37.296 --> 02:41.396 align:middle
in participation among
individuals aged 25 to 54 years

02:41.816 --> 02:44.226 align:middle
and a continued strong
pace of immigration.

02:46.246 --> 02:49.106 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
eased over the past year

02:49.106 --> 02:50.996 align:middle
and the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed,

02:51.816 --> 02:55.016 align:middle
but labor demand still exceeds
the supply of available workers.

02:57.036 --> 02:59.916 align:middle
Inflation has eased notably
over the past year but remains

03:00.006 --> 03:02.206 align:middle
above our longer-run
goal of 2 percent.

03:03.186 --> 03:06.166 align:middle
Total PCE prices
rose 2.7 percent

03:06.166 --> 03:07.776 align:middle
over the 12 months
ending in March;

03:08.586 --> 03:10.896 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:10.936 --> 03:13.746 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.8 percent.

03:14.656 --> 03:16.656 align:middle
The inflation data received

03:16.656 --> 03:18.776 align:middle
so far this year have
been higher than expected.

03:19.536 --> 03:20.436 align:middle
Although some measures

03:20.436 --> 03:22.776 align:middle
of short-term inflation
expectations have increased

03:22.776 --> 03:26.496 align:middle
in recent months, longer-term
inflation expectations appear

03:26.496 --> 03:29.546 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

03:29.546 --> 03:32.646 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

03:33.326 --> 03:35.226 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

03:37.056 --> 03:39.366 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

03:39.366 --> 03:41.766 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

03:41.966 --> 03:43.926 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

03:45.116 --> 03:46.956 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

03:47.096 --> 03:49.736 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

03:50.206 --> 03:54.416 align:middle
as it erodes purchasing power,
especially for those least able

03:54.416 --> 03:57.006 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

03:57.286 --> 03:58.656 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

03:58.776 --> 04:01.966 align:middle
We are strongly committed
to returning inflation

04:01.966 --> 04:03.506 align:middle
to our 2 percent objective.

04:05.376 --> 04:06.986 align:middle
The Committee decided
at today's meeting

04:06.986 --> 04:09.996 align:middle
to maintain the target range for
the federal funds rate at 5¼

04:09.996 --> 04:13.346 align:middle
to 5½ percent and to
continue the process

04:13.346 --> 04:15.646 align:middle
of significantly reducing
our securities holdings,

04:16.046 --> 04:17.376 align:middle
though at a slower pace.

04:19.296 --> 04:22.266 align:middle
Over the past year, as labor
market tightness has eased

04:23.236 --> 04:24.686 align:middle
and inflation has declined,

04:24.986 --> 04:26.646 align:middle
the risks to achieving
our employment

04:26.646 --> 04:29.866 align:middle
and inflation goals have
moved toward better balance.

04:31.016 --> 04:32.926 align:middle
The economic outlook
is uncertain, however,

04:33.106 --> 04:35.676 align:middle
and we remain highly
attentive to inflation risks.

04:37.296 --> 04:38.906 align:middle
We've stated that
we do not expect

04:38.906 --> 04:41.186 align:middle
that it will be appropriate
to reduce the target range

04:41.186 --> 04:42.146 align:middle
for the federal funds rate

04:42.856 --> 04:44.816 align:middle
until we have gained
greater confidence

04:44.816 --> 04:47.876 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably toward 2 percent.

04:48.966 --> 04:51.056 align:middle
So far this year, the
data have not given us

04:51.096 --> 04:52.126 align:middle
that greater confidence.

04:52.636 --> 04:54.406 align:middle
In particular, and
as I noted earlier,

04:54.896 --> 04:57.826 align:middle
readings on inflation have
come in above expectations.

04:58.536 --> 04:59.826 align:middle
It is likely that gaining

04:59.826 --> 05:01.866 align:middle
such greater confidence
will take longer

05:02.016 --> 05:03.456 align:middle
than previously expected.

05:04.656 --> 05:06.676 align:middle
We are prepared to maintain
the current target range

05:06.676 --> 05:09.136 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
for as long as appropriate.

05:10.246 --> 05:11.616 align:middle
We're also prepared to respond

05:11.616 --> 05:13.786 align:middle
to an unexpected weakening
in the labor market.

05:15.736 --> 05:18.596 align:middle
We know that reducing
policy restraint too soon

05:18.596 --> 05:21.036 align:middle
or too much could
result in a reversal

05:21.036 --> 05:22.646 align:middle
of the progress we've
seen on inflation.

05:23.796 --> 05:26.526 align:middle
At the same time, reducing
policy restraint too late

05:26.526 --> 05:29.606 align:middle
or too little could unduly
weaken economic activity

05:29.866 --> 05:30.496 align:middle
and employment.

05:31.796 --> 05:34.036 align:middle
In considering any
adjustments to the target range

05:34.036 --> 05:35.026 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

05:35.236 --> 05:37.556 align:middle
the Committee will carefully
assess incoming data,

05:38.076 --> 05:40.476 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

05:41.686 --> 05:44.166 align:middle
Policy is well positioned
to deal with the risks

05:44.166 --> 05:45.526 align:middle
and uncertainties that we face

05:45.626 --> 05:48.146 align:middle
in pursuing both sides
of our dual mandate.

05:48.856 --> 05:52.366 align:middle
We will continue to make
decisions meeting by meeting.

05:54.266 --> 05:56.776 align:middle
Turning to our balance
sheet: The Committee decided

05:56.776 --> 05:59.016 align:middle
at today's meeting to
slow the pace of decline

05:59.016 --> 06:00.146 align:middle
in our securities holdings,

06:00.666 --> 06:03.086 align:middle
consistent with the plans
we released previously.

06:04.616 --> 06:05.526 align:middle
Specifically, the cap

06:05.526 --> 06:07.306 align:middle
on Treasury redemptions
will be lowered

06:07.306 --> 06:09.106 align:middle
from the current $60
billion per month

06:09.526 --> 06:11.836 align:middle
to $25 billion per
month as of June 1.

06:13.076 --> 06:14.516 align:middle
Consistent with the
Committee's intention

06:14.516 --> 06:17.186 align:middle
to hold primarily Treasury
securities in the longer run,

06:17.776 --> 06:21.266 align:middle
we're leaving the cap on agency
securities unchanged per month,

06:21.576 --> 06:24.836 align:middle
and we will reinvest any
proceeds in excess of this cap

06:24.836 --> 06:25.886 align:middle
in Treasury securities.

06:27.346 --> 06:28.236 align:middle
With principal payments

06:28.236 --> 06:30.066 align:middle
on agency securities
currently running

06:30.066 --> 06:32.386 align:middle
at about $15 billion per month,

06:32.716 --> 06:34.596 align:middle
total portfolio runoff
will amount

06:34.596 --> 06:36.726 align:middle
to roughly $40 billion
per month.

06:38.326 --> 06:40.796 align:middle
The decision to slow the
pace of runoff does not mean

06:40.796 --> 06:42.946 align:middle
that our balance sheet will
ultimately shrink by less

06:43.006 --> 06:45.166 align:middle
than it would otherwise
but rather allows us

06:45.166 --> 06:47.556 align:middle
to approach its ultimate
level more gradually.

06:48.616 --> 06:50.406 align:middle
In particular, slowing the pace

06:50.406 --> 06:52.896 align:middle
of runoff will help
ensure a smooth transition,

06:53.466 --> 06:56.616 align:middle
reducing the possibility that
money markets experience stress

06:57.266 --> 06:59.616 align:middle
and thereby facilitating
the ongoing decline

06:59.616 --> 07:02.806 align:middle
in our securities holdings
that are consistent

07:02.806 --> 07:05.096 align:middle
with reaching the appropriate
level of ample reserves.

07:06.376 --> 07:08.856 align:middle
We remain committed to
bringing inflation back

07:08.856 --> 07:10.146 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal

07:10.606 --> 07:13.016 align:middle
and to keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

07:13.106 --> 07:13.736 align:middle
well anchored.

07:14.846 --> 07:17.256 align:middle
Restoring price stability is
essential to set the stage

07:17.256 --> 07:18.676 align:middle
for achieving maximum employment

07:18.766 --> 07:21.046 align:middle
and stable prices
over the longer run.

07:22.396 --> 07:25.566 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:25.696 --> 07:27.836 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:29.066 --> 07:31.796 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:32.876 --> 07:34.776 align:middle
We, at the Fed, will
do everything we can

07:34.926 --> 07:36.906 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:37.026 --> 07:38.176 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:39.006 --> 07:39.326 align:middle
Thank you.

07:39.496 --> 07:40.926 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

07:41.226 --> 07:43.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:43.886 --> 07:45.006 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

07:45.076 --> 07:45.296 align:middle
Thank you.

07:45.296 --> 07:46.736 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

07:46.736 --> 07:49.056 align:middle
A question and follow-up
if I could,

07:49.056 --> 07:52.676 align:middle
do you consider the current
policy rate still-are you

07:52.706 --> 07:54.556 align:middle
confident that it's
sufficiently restrictive

07:55.206 --> 07:56.776 align:middle
to get inflation
back to 2 percent?

07:57.376 --> 07:57.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:57.716 --> 08:01.166 align:middle
So I do think the evidence
shows, you know, pretty clearly

08:01.166 --> 08:03.556 align:middle
that policy is restrictive
and is weighing on demand,

08:04.236 --> 08:06.956 align:middle
and there are a few places
I would point to for that.

08:07.466 --> 08:08.866 align:middle
You can start with
the labor market.

08:09.016 --> 08:13.426 align:middle
So demand is still strong-the
demand side of the labor market,

08:13.426 --> 08:16.596 align:middle
in particular-but it's cooled
from its extremely high level

08:16.596 --> 08:19.296 align:middle
of a couple years ago, and
you see that in job openings.

08:19.296 --> 08:21.146 align:middle
You saw it-more evidence
of that today

08:21.146 --> 08:23.386 align:middle
in the JOLTS report,
as you'll know.

08:23.386 --> 08:24.926 align:middle
It's still higher
than pre-pandemic.

08:24.926 --> 08:26.756 align:middle
But it has been coming down both

08:26.756 --> 08:28.586 align:middle
in the Indeed report
and the JOLTS report.

08:28.986 --> 08:30.576 align:middle
That's, that's demand cooling.

08:31.156 --> 08:33.516 align:middle
The same is true of
quits and hiring rates,

08:33.516 --> 08:36.446 align:middle
which have essentially
normalized.

08:36.446 --> 08:39.116 align:middle
I also look at the-we
look at surveys

08:39.116 --> 08:42.446 align:middle
of workers and-pardon me-surveys
of workers and businesses,

08:42.446 --> 08:44.796 align:middle
and [they] ask workers,
"Are jobs plentiful?"

08:44.796 --> 08:47.276 align:middle
and ask businesses,
"Are workers plentiful?

08:47.276 --> 08:48.406 align:middle
Is it easy to find workers?"

08:48.576 --> 08:51.226 align:middle
And you've seen that the
answers to those have come back

08:51.226 --> 08:53.976 align:middle
down to pre-pandemic levels.

08:54.376 --> 08:57.246 align:middle
You also see in
interest-sensitive spending,

08:57.246 --> 08:59.516 align:middle
like housing and
investment-you also see

08:59.516 --> 09:02.596 align:middle
that higher interest rates are
weighing on those activities.

09:02.596 --> 09:06.256 align:middle
So I do think it's clear that,
that policy is restrictive.

09:06.256 --> 09:06.976 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:06.976 --> 09:08.446 align:middle
Sufficiently restrictive,
I guess.

09:08.666 --> 09:09.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:09.686 --> 09:13.746 align:middle
So I would say that we
believe it is restrictive,

09:13.746 --> 09:15.186 align:middle
and we believe [that]
over time it will be

09:15.186 --> 09:16.196 align:middle
sufficiently restrictive.

09:16.476 --> 09:17.526 align:middle
That will be a question that,

09:17.526 --> 09:19.016 align:middle
that the data will
have to answer.

09:19.136 --> 09:19.496 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:19.496 --> 09:22.796 align:middle
So as a follow-up, if inflation
continues running roughly

09:22.796 --> 09:27.636 align:middle
sideways as it has been, the job
market stays reasonably strong,

09:27.636 --> 09:30.606 align:middle
unemployment low, and
expectations are anchored

09:30.606 --> 09:33.776 align:middle
and maintained, would you
disrupt that for- CHAIR POWELL.

09:33.776 --> 09:34.746 align:middle
Expectations are not anchored?

09:34.746 --> 09:35.046 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:35.046 --> 09:35.606 align:middle
Are anchored.

09:35.606 --> 09:35.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:35.826 --> 09:36.116 align:middle
Are anchored.

09:36.186 --> 09:36.606 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:36.606 --> 09:37.546 align:middle
Stable, roughly.

09:38.046 --> 09:41.236 align:middle
Would you disrupt that for
the last half point on PCE?

09:41.236 --> 09:41.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:41.746 --> 09:44.646 align:middle
You know, I don't want to get
into complicated hypotheticals.

09:44.706 --> 09:46.876 align:middle
But I would say that,
you know, we're committed

09:46.876 --> 09:50.806 align:middle
to retaining our current
restrictive stance of policy

09:50.876 --> 09:52.186 align:middle
for as long as is appropriate.

09:52.246 --> 09:52.886 align:middle
And we'll do that.

09:55.436 --> 09:57.276 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:58.036 --> 09:58.886 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

09:59.786 --> 10:02.176 align:middle
Thanks for taking our
questions, Chair Powell.

10:02.586 --> 10:06.986 align:middle
I wonder-you know, obviously
Michelle Bowman has been saying

10:06.986 --> 10:09.876 align:middle
that there is a risk that rates
may need to increase further,

10:09.876 --> 10:11.016 align:middle
although it's not
her baseline outlook.

10:11.016 --> 10:14.766 align:middle
I wonder if you see that as
a risk as well, and, if so,

10:14.886 --> 10:18.216 align:middle
what change in conditions would
merit considering raising rates

10:18.216 --> 10:18.746 align:middle
at this point?

10:18.826 --> 10:19.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:19.786 --> 10:21.316 align:middle
So I think it's unlikely

10:22.136 --> 10:24.676 align:middle
that the next policy
rate move will be a hike.

10:24.866 --> 10:25.876 align:middle
I'd say it's unlikely.

10:26.436 --> 10:30.516 align:middle
You know, our policy focus is
really what I just mentioned,

10:30.516 --> 10:33.526 align:middle
which is-which is how long
to keep policy restrictive.

10:34.356 --> 10:35.646 align:middle
You ask, what would it take?

10:35.646 --> 10:38.366 align:middle
You know, I think we'd need
to see persuasive evidence

10:38.366 --> 10:40.886 align:middle
that our policy stance is
not sufficiently restrictive

10:40.886 --> 10:43.296 align:middle
to bring inflation sustainably
down to 2 percent over time.

10:43.726 --> 10:45.736 align:middle
That's not-that's not what
we think we're seeing,

10:45.736 --> 10:47.646 align:middle
as I-as I mentioned,
but that's-something

10:47.646 --> 10:48.706 align:middle
like that is what it would take.

10:49.196 --> 10:51.746 align:middle
We'd look at the totality of the
data in answer to that question.

10:51.746 --> 10:55.316 align:middle
That would include inflation,
inflation expectations,

10:55.316 --> 10:56.056 align:middle
and all the other data too.

10:56.056 --> 10:57.666 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

10:59.626 --> 11:01.386 align:middle
Would that be-would that be a
reacceleration in inflation?

11:01.386 --> 11:01.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:03.996 --> 11:07.526 align:middle
Well, I think, again, the
test-what I'm saying is,

11:07.526 --> 11:09.216 align:middle
if we were to come
to that conclusion

11:09.216 --> 11:11.526 align:middle
that policy weren't tight
enough to achieve that,

11:11.916 --> 11:13.376 align:middle
so it would be the totality

11:13.376 --> 11:15.146 align:middle
of all the things
we'd be looking at.

11:15.146 --> 11:16.446 align:middle
It could be expectations.

11:16.446 --> 11:17.826 align:middle
It could be a combination
of things.

11:17.856 --> 11:19.316 align:middle
But, if we-if we reach

11:19.316 --> 11:21.646 align:middle
that conclusion-and we don't
see evidence supporting

11:21.646 --> 11:23.906 align:middle
that conclusion-that's
what it would take I think

11:23.906 --> 11:25.366 align:middle
for us to take that step.

11:25.366 --> 11:26.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:26.926 --> 11:28.586 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

11:29.436 --> 11:32.556 align:middle
Thank you, Chris Rugaber
at Associated Press.

11:32.556 --> 11:35.236 align:middle
You didn't mention the idea
that rates are at a peak

11:35.316 --> 11:37.686 align:middle
for the cycle and
didn't mention the idea

11:37.686 --> 11:39.966 align:middle
that it might be appropriate
to cut rates later this year

11:40.486 --> 11:42.436 align:middle
as you have in previous
press conferences.

11:42.436 --> 11:45.766 align:middle
So has the Fed sort of
dropped its easing bias?

11:45.866 --> 11:46.916 align:middle
Where are you standing on that?

11:47.166 --> 11:47.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:47.746 --> 11:51.876 align:middle
So, on-let me address cuts.

11:51.936 --> 11:54.606 align:middle
So, obviously, our
decisions that we make

11:54.606 --> 11:57.126 align:middle
on our policy rate are going
to depend on the incoming data,

11:57.666 --> 12:00.696 align:middle
how the outlook is evolving, and
the balance of risks, as always.

12:00.746 --> 12:02.516 align:middle
And we'll look at the
totality of the data.

12:03.246 --> 12:06.496 align:middle
So I think, and we think,
that policy is well positioned

12:06.496 --> 12:09.996 align:middle
to address different paths
that the economy might take.

12:10.706 --> 12:13.296 align:middle
And we've said that we don't
think it would be appropriate

12:13.296 --> 12:15.296 align:middle
to dial back our
restrictive policy stance

12:15.296 --> 12:16.926 align:middle
until we've gained
greater confidence

12:16.986 --> 12:19.586 align:middle
that inflation is moving down
sustainably toward 2 percent.

12:20.016 --> 12:25.536 align:middle
So, for example, let me take
a path: If we did have a path

12:25.596 --> 12:28.396 align:middle
where inflation proves more
persistent than expected

12:28.396 --> 12:30.676 align:middle
and where the labor
market remains strong,

12:31.116 --> 12:33.276 align:middle
but inflation is moving sideways

12:33.276 --> 12:35.146 align:middle
and we're not gaining
greater confidence, well,

12:35.146 --> 12:37.846 align:middle
that would be a case in
which it could be appropriate

12:37.846 --> 12:39.036 align:middle
to hold off on rate cuts.

12:39.756 --> 12:42.806 align:middle
I think there's also other paths
that the economy could take,

12:42.956 --> 12:45.896 align:middle
which, which would cause us
to want to consider rate cuts.

12:45.896 --> 12:48.376 align:middle
And those would be-two
of those paths would be

12:49.006 --> 12:51.216 align:middle
that we do gain greater
confidence, as we've said,

12:51.996 --> 12:54.556 align:middle
that inflation is moving
sustainably down to 2 percent,

12:54.556 --> 12:58.456 align:middle
and another path could be, you
know, an unexpected weakening

12:58.456 --> 12:59.876 align:middle
in the labor market,
for example.

12:59.876 --> 13:05.246 align:middle
So those are paths in which
you could see us cutting rates.

13:05.246 --> 13:07.506 align:middle
So I think it really
will depend on the data.

13:07.976 --> 13:12.326 align:middle
In terms of peak rate,
you know, I think, really,

13:12.326 --> 13:13.326 align:middle
it's the same question.

13:13.466 --> 13:17.716 align:middle
I, I think the data will have
to answer that question for us.

13:18.466 --> 13:18.886 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

13:18.886 --> 13:21.896 align:middle
And could you just follow-on the
path where you might not cut,

13:21.896 --> 13:27.466 align:middle
is that-you mentioned that would
be inflation persistent-I mean,

13:27.466 --> 13:29.456 align:middle
is inflation-would that
be the key data in making

13:29.456 --> 13:31.866 align:middle
that decision, or could you
expand a bit more on that?

13:31.966 --> 13:32.306 align:middle
Thank you.

13:32.446 --> 13:33.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:33.076 --> 13:38.116 align:middle
Again, it's, it's-we've set
ourselves a test that we-for us

13:38.116 --> 13:40.866 align:middle
to begin to reduce
policy restriction,

13:41.376 --> 13:43.846 align:middle
we'd want to be confident that
inflation is moving-you know,

13:43.846 --> 13:47.396 align:middle
moving sustainably down to
2 percent, and, for sure,

13:47.396 --> 13:48.396 align:middle
one of the things
we'd be looking

13:48.396 --> 13:49.866 align:middle
at is the performance
of inflation.

13:49.986 --> 13:51.986 align:middle
We'd also be looking at
inflation expectations.

13:51.986 --> 13:53.076 align:middle
We'd be looking at
the whole story.

13:53.076 --> 13:57.306 align:middle
But clearly, incoming,
incoming inflation data would be

13:57.306 --> 13:58.476 align:middle
at the very heart
of that decision.

13:58.476 --> 13:59.056 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

13:59.056 --> 14:00.296 align:middle
Thank you.

14:01.086 --> 14:01.836 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:03.476 --> 14:04.976 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

14:07.206 --> 14:08.516 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

14:09.046 --> 14:12.126 align:middle
Chair Powell, to what
extent has the easing

14:12.126 --> 14:15.016 align:middle
in financial conditions
since November contributed

14:15.436 --> 14:19.146 align:middle
to the reacceleration in growth,
and do you now expect a period

14:19.146 --> 14:23.236 align:middle
of sustained tighter financial
conditions will be needed

14:23.236 --> 14:24.386 align:middle
to resume the sort

14:24.386 --> 14:26.926 align:middle
of disinflation the
economy saw last year?

14:26.926 --> 14:27.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:27.986 --> 14:29.826 align:middle
So I think it's hard
to know that.

14:29.826 --> 14:31.806 align:middle
I think we'll be able
to look back, you know,

14:31.996 --> 14:35.186 align:middle
from down the road and look
back and understand it better.

14:35.576 --> 14:39.016 align:middle
You know, if you look
at-let's look at growth.

14:39.056 --> 14:42.356 align:middle
Really, what we've
seen so far this year

14:42.356 --> 14:46.396 align:middle
in the first quarter is growth
coming in about consistent

14:46.396 --> 14:47.816 align:middle
with where it was last year.

14:47.816 --> 14:49.346 align:middle
I know GDP came in lower,

14:49.726 --> 14:51.336 align:middle
but you don't see an
acceleration in growth.

14:51.336 --> 14:52.256 align:middle
I mean, the thought would be

14:52.256 --> 14:55.926 align:middle
that financial conditions
loosened in, in, December,

14:56.256 --> 14:58.256 align:middle
and that caused an
uptick in activity,

14:58.256 --> 14:59.506 align:middle
and that caused inflation.

14:59.506 --> 15:01.696 align:middle
Presumably, that's what we're
tightening in the labor market.

15:02.116 --> 15:03.536 align:middle
You don't really
see that happening.

15:03.536 --> 15:05.996 align:middle
What you see is economic
activity

15:06.366 --> 15:09.116 align:middle
at a level that's roughly
the same as, as last year.

15:09.116 --> 15:11.716 align:middle
So, you know, what's
causing this inflation?

15:12.316 --> 15:14.756 align:middle
You know, we'll, we'll have a
better sense of that over time.

15:14.756 --> 15:16.786 align:middle
I don't know that there's
an obvious connection there,

15:16.786 --> 15:18.426 align:middle
though, with easing of
financial conditions.

15:18.836 --> 15:20.746 align:middle
In terms of tightening,
you're, you're right.

15:20.746 --> 15:23.836 align:middle
Rates are certainly higher now
and have been for some time

15:24.366 --> 15:26.326 align:middle
than they were before
the December meeting.

15:26.376 --> 15:28.956 align:middle
And they're higher, and that's
tighter financial conditions.

15:28.956 --> 15:30.916 align:middle
And, you know, that's
appropriate,

15:31.136 --> 15:33.296 align:middle
given what inflation has
done in the first quarter.

15:33.886 --> 15:34.396 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

15:34.396 --> 15:35.246 align:middle
You've said in the past

15:35.306 --> 15:38.556 align:middle
that stronger growth wouldn't
necessarily preclude rate cuts.

15:38.886 --> 15:40.376 align:middle
I wonder, would continued
strength

15:40.376 --> 15:42.456 align:middle
in the labor market
change your view

15:42.456 --> 15:45.016 align:middle
about the appropriate stance
of policy if it was accompanied

15:45.016 --> 15:47.676 align:middle
by signs the wage growth
was reaccelerating?

15:47.956 --> 15:48.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:48.476 --> 15:49.696 align:middle
So I just want to be careful

15:49.696 --> 15:52.226 align:middle
that we don't target wage
growth or the labor market.

15:52.266 --> 15:55.316 align:middle
And remember what we saw last
year: very strong growth,

15:55.556 --> 15:56.876 align:middle
a really tight labor market,

15:57.276 --> 15:59.946 align:middle
and a historically fast
decline in inflation.

16:00.416 --> 16:03.936 align:middle
So-and that's because we
know there are two forces

16:03.936 --> 16:04.416 align:middle
at work here.

16:04.416 --> 16:05.396 align:middle
There's the unwinding

16:05.396 --> 16:08.556 align:middle
of the pandemic-related
supply-side distortions

16:08.776 --> 16:12.996 align:middle
and demand-side distortions, and
there's also monetary policy,

16:13.056 --> 16:16.006 align:middle
you know, restrictive
monetary policy.

16:16.006 --> 16:19.016 align:middle
So I wouldn't rule
out that something

16:19.016 --> 16:20.596 align:middle
like that can continue.

16:20.726 --> 16:22.796 align:middle
You know, I wouldn't
give up, at this point,

16:22.796 --> 16:26.076 align:middle
on further things happening on
the supply side either because,

16:26.146 --> 16:29.156 align:middle
you know, we do see that
companies still report

16:29.636 --> 16:32.336 align:middle
that there are supply-side
issues that they're facing.

16:32.336 --> 16:35.866 align:middle
And also, even when the
supply-side issues are solved,

16:36.016 --> 16:38.806 align:middle
it should take some time for
that to affect economic activity

16:38.806 --> 16:39.866 align:middle
and ultimately inflation.

16:40.206 --> 16:41.326 align:middle
So there are still those things.

16:41.646 --> 16:46.136 align:middle
So I don't like to say that
either strong-either growth or,

16:46.596 --> 16:49.036 align:middle
or a strong labor
market, in and of itself,

16:49.676 --> 16:52.546 align:middle
would automatically create
problems on inflation, because,

16:52.546 --> 16:53.946 align:middle
of course, it didn't
do that last year.

16:54.486 --> 16:55.406 align:middle
You ask about wages.

16:55.406 --> 16:58.616 align:middle
We also don't-we
don't target wages.

16:58.616 --> 17:00.496 align:middle
We target price inflation.

17:01.006 --> 17:02.296 align:middle
It is one of the inputs.

17:02.296 --> 17:05.696 align:middle
The point with wages is, of
course, we, like everyone else,

17:05.776 --> 17:09.136 align:middle
like to see high
wages, but we also want

17:09.136 --> 17:12.046 align:middle
to see them not eaten
up by high inflation.

17:12.126 --> 17:14.016 align:middle
And that's really what
we're trying to do,

17:14.016 --> 17:18.656 align:middle
is to cool the economy and
work with what's happening

17:18.656 --> 17:22.256 align:middle
on the supply side to
bring-to bring the economy back

17:22.256 --> 17:23.446 align:middle
to 2 percent inflation.

17:23.446 --> 17:28.186 align:middle
Part of that will probably
be having wage increases move

17:28.366 --> 17:31.296 align:middle
down incrementally toward levels
that are more sustainable.

17:33.156 --> 17:34.196 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:34.196 --> 17:36.566 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

17:36.806 --> 17:37.556 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

17:37.556 --> 17:38.916 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

17:38.996 --> 17:40.096 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

17:40.696 --> 17:43.396 align:middle
You talked about needing
time to gain more confidence

17:43.396 --> 17:46.096 align:middle
that inflation is sustainably
moving back down to 2 percent.

17:47.146 --> 17:47.906 align:middle
It's May now.

17:47.906 --> 17:50.646 align:middle
Do you have time this
year to cut three times,

17:50.646 --> 17:51.416 align:middle
just given the calendar?

17:51.416 --> 17:52.486 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:52.566 --> 17:55.486 align:middle
Yeah. I'm not really
thinking of it that way.

17:55.676 --> 17:58.816 align:middle
You know, the-what we
said is that we need

17:58.816 --> 18:01.506 align:middle
to be more confident, and
we've said-my colleagues

18:01.506 --> 18:05.656 align:middle
and I today said that
we didn't see progress

18:05.656 --> 18:08.596 align:middle
in the first-in the
first quarter.

18:08.836 --> 18:12.466 align:middle
And I've said that it
appears, then, that it's going

18:12.466 --> 18:14.846 align:middle
to take longer for us to reach
that point of confidence.

18:14.846 --> 18:16.866 align:middle
So I don't know how
long it'll take.

18:16.866 --> 18:21.046 align:middle
You know, I can just say that
when we get that confidence,

18:21.116 --> 18:23.686 align:middle
then rate cuts will be in scope.

18:24.296 --> 18:26.416 align:middle
And I don't know exactly
when that will be.

18:26.416 --> 18:27.146 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

18:27.146 --> 18:29.796 align:middle
And, with hindsight, are there
any signs that you can look back

18:29.796 --> 18:32.576 align:middle
on now, looking at the reports
from January or February

18:32.576 --> 18:35.076 align:middle
or March, that suggested
something more worrying

18:35.076 --> 18:36.546 align:middle
than just expected bumpiness?

18:41.426 --> 18:42.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:42.116 --> 18:43.416 align:middle
I-you know, not really.

18:43.416 --> 18:46.996 align:middle
You know, what, what-so I
thought it was appropriate

18:46.996 --> 18:50.086 align:middle
to reserve judgment until, until
we had the full quarter's data,

18:50.086 --> 18:51.236 align:middle
until we saw the March data.

18:51.236 --> 18:52.476 align:middle
And so take a step back.

18:52.476 --> 18:54.506 align:middle
What do we now see
in the first quarter?

18:54.506 --> 18:57.576 align:middle
We see strong economic activity,

18:57.576 --> 18:59.736 align:middle
we see a strong labor
market, and we see inflation.

18:59.736 --> 19:03.606 align:middle
We see three inflation
readings, and so I think you're

19:03.606 --> 19:06.596 align:middle
at a point there where you
should take some signal.

19:07.316 --> 19:09.476 align:middle
We don't like to react to
one or two months' data,

19:09.476 --> 19:10.526 align:middle
but this is a full quarter.

19:10.766 --> 19:12.546 align:middle
And I think it's appropriate
to take signal now,

19:12.546 --> 19:13.596 align:middle
and we are taking signal.

19:13.596 --> 19:16.336 align:middle
And the signal that we're
taking is that it's likely

19:16.336 --> 19:19.836 align:middle
to take longer for us to
gain confidence that we are

19:20.046 --> 19:22.276 align:middle
on a sustainable path down
to 2 percent inflation.

19:22.726 --> 19:24.266 align:middle
That's the signal
that we're taking now.

19:24.386 --> 19:25.886 align:middle
Yeah. MICHELLE SMITH.

19:26.536 --> 19:28.836 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

19:28.966 --> 19:30.906 align:middle
Mr. Chair, if I could-Steve
Liesman, CNBC.

19:30.906 --> 19:36.166 align:middle
If I could follow up on that:
What particular areas were sort

19:36.166 --> 19:37.916 align:middle
of temporary or blips

19:37.916 --> 19:39.536 align:middle
in the inflation data
in the first quarter?

19:39.986 --> 19:42.166 align:middle
What's the dynamic by which
you expect them to work

19:42.166 --> 19:44.246 align:middle
out in the coming
months and quarters?

19:44.246 --> 19:44.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:45.046 --> 19:47.286 align:middle
Yeah. So we will-you know,

19:47.286 --> 19:49.336 align:middle
we will put the thing-we
have put the thing

19:49.336 --> 19:50.216 align:middle
under a microscope.

19:50.216 --> 19:52.336 align:middle
I will say, nothing is
going to come out of

19:52.336 --> 19:53.916 align:middle
that that's going
to change the view.

19:53.916 --> 19:57.906 align:middle
I think that, in fact,
we didn't gain confidence

19:57.906 --> 19:59.076 align:middle
and that it's going
to take longer to get

19:59.076 --> 20:00.806 align:middle
that confidence-but-confidence.

20:00.846 --> 20:03.826 align:middle
I just think-you know the story.

20:03.826 --> 20:09.126 align:middle
What's happened since December
is you've seen higher goods

20:09.126 --> 20:10.326 align:middle
inflation than expected,

20:10.426 --> 20:13.066 align:middle
and you've seen higher
nonhousing services inflation

20:13.066 --> 20:13.576 align:middle
than expected.

20:13.576 --> 20:15.116 align:middle
And those two are
working together to,

20:15.576 --> 20:17.846 align:middle
to sort of be higher
than we had thought.

20:17.846 --> 20:22.216 align:middle
And there are stories behind how
that happened, and, you know,

20:22.216 --> 20:25.596 align:middle
we-I think-I think my
expectation is that we will,

20:25.766 --> 20:29.306 align:middle
over the course of this year,
see inflation move back down.

20:29.376 --> 20:31.376 align:middle
That's, that's my forecast.

20:31.956 --> 20:35.966 align:middle
I think my confidence in
that is lower than it was

20:36.526 --> 20:38.176 align:middle
because of the data
that we've seen.

20:38.546 --> 20:40.006 align:middle
So, you know, we're
looking at those things.

20:40.006 --> 20:42.646 align:middle
We also continue to expect,
and I continue to expect,

20:42.646 --> 20:45.876 align:middle
that housing services inflation,
given where market rents are,

20:46.776 --> 20:51.496 align:middle
those will show up in measured
housing services inflation

20:51.496 --> 20:52.066 align:middle
over time.

20:52.746 --> 20:54.326 align:middle
We believe that it will.

20:54.326 --> 20:56.766 align:middle
It just-it looks like the
lag-that there are substantial

20:56.766 --> 21:00.356 align:middle
lags between when, you know,
lower market rates turned

21:00.356 --> 21:05.716 align:middle
up and-for new tenants and when
it shows up for existing tenants

21:05.716 --> 21:07.576 align:middle
or for in-housing services.

21:08.146 --> 21:08.806 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

21:08.996 --> 21:11.376 align:middle
If I could just follow up: Is
there a bit of a contradiction

21:11.376 --> 21:14.696 align:middle
in the idea that you are
reducing quantitative

21:14.746 --> 21:16.636 align:middle
tightening, which is
sort of an easing,

21:16.636 --> 21:19.916 align:middle
while you're holding rates
steady at a restrictive rate

21:19.916 --> 21:21.776 align:middle
to try to slow and cool
the economy and inflation?

21:21.836 --> 21:22.466 align:middle
Thank you.

21:22.626 --> 21:23.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:23.206 --> 21:23.936 align:middle
I wouldn't say that.

21:23.936 --> 21:26.326 align:middle
No. I mean, the active
tool of monetary policy is,

21:26.326 --> 21:27.486 align:middle
of course, the interest rates.

21:27.576 --> 21:31.786 align:middle
And this is-this is a long-a
plan we've long had in place

21:31.786 --> 21:39.496 align:middle
to slow, really not in order to,
you know, provide accommodation

21:39.496 --> 21:44.426 align:middle
to the economy but to-or to be
less restrictive to the economy.

21:44.426 --> 21:47.216 align:middle
It really is to ensure
that the process

21:47.326 --> 21:49.186 align:middle
of shrinking the balance
sheet down to where we want

21:49.186 --> 21:53.226 align:middle
to get it is a smooth
one and doesn't wind

21:53.226 --> 21:56.656 align:middle
up in-with financial market
turmoil the way it did the

21:56.656 --> 21:58.336 align:middle
last-the last time we did this

21:58.336 --> 21:59.816 align:middle
and the only other time
we've ever done this.

22:01.176 --> 22:03.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:03.976 --> 22:05.026 align:middle
Craig. CRAIG TORRES.

22:05.086 --> 22:06.296 align:middle
Craig Torres from Bloomberg.

22:08.106 --> 22:08.786 align:middle
Two questions.

22:08.786 --> 22:10.386 align:middle
First, a simple one.

22:10.476 --> 22:14.496 align:middle
Given the run of data since
March, has the probability

22:14.496 --> 22:17.246 align:middle
in your mind of no cuts
this year increased

22:17.296 --> 22:18.386 align:middle
or stayed the same?

22:19.206 --> 22:20.326 align:middle
That's the first question.

22:21.246 --> 22:22.156 align:middle
Second question.

22:23.136 --> 22:25.556 align:middle
Chair Powell, you
joined the Board in 2012,

22:25.786 --> 22:27.846 align:middle
and I'm sure you
remember, as I do,

22:27.846 --> 22:32.436 align:middle
what the jobless recovery was
like: lawyers, accountants,

22:32.896 --> 22:36.676 align:middle
all kinds of highly qualified
people who couldn't get jobs.

22:37.256 --> 22:40.036 align:middle
And given your history there,
I wonder if there's an argument

22:40.036 --> 22:43.486 align:middle
for being more patient
with inflation here.

22:43.866 --> 22:48.876 align:middle
We have strong productivity
growth that's helping wages grow

22:48.876 --> 22:50.326 align:middle
up-go up.

22:50.326 --> 22:51.776 align:middle
We have good employment.

22:52.426 --> 22:55.856 align:middle
And so it seems to me there's a
lot of hysteria about inflation.

22:55.856 --> 23:00.536 align:middle
I agree-you know, nobody likes
it, but is there an argument

23:00.536 --> 23:03.856 align:middle
for being patient and working
with the economic cycle

23:03.856 --> 23:05.256 align:middle
to get it down over time?

23:05.356 --> 23:05.866 align:middle
Thank you.

23:06.016 --> 23:06.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:06.756 --> 23:07.896 align:middle
So, on your first question,

23:07.896 --> 23:09.746 align:middle
I don't have a probability
estimate for you.

23:09.746 --> 23:14.726 align:middle
But all I can say
is that, you know,

23:14.936 --> 23:17.616 align:middle
we've said that we didn't think
it would be appropriate to cut

23:17.616 --> 23:18.916 align:middle
until we were more confident

23:19.526 --> 23:22.106 align:middle
that inflation was moving
sustainably down to 2 percent.

23:22.186 --> 23:23.716 align:middle
We didn't get our confidence,

23:23.716 --> 23:25.896 align:middle
in that [it] didn't increase
in the first quarter.

23:25.896 --> 23:30.146 align:middle
And, in fact, what really
happened was we came to the view

23:30.146 --> 23:32.076 align:middle
that it will take longer
to get that confidence.

23:32.076 --> 23:34.056 align:middle
And I think there are-you know,

23:34.056 --> 23:37.006 align:middle
I think it's-the economy
has been very hard

23:37.006 --> 23:41.246 align:middle
for forecasters broadly to
predict-to predict its path.

23:41.316 --> 23:44.416 align:middle
But there are paths
to, to not cutting,

23:44.416 --> 23:45.426 align:middle
and there are paths to cutting.

23:45.426 --> 23:47.656 align:middle
It's really going to
depend on the data.

23:47.656 --> 23:52.286 align:middle
In terms of the employment
mandate, to your point,

23:52.346 --> 23:54.086 align:middle
if you go back a
couple of years, our,

23:54.376 --> 24:00.246 align:middle
our sort of framework document
says that, when you look

24:00.246 --> 24:03.246 align:middle
at the two mandate goals, and
if one of them is further away

24:03.246 --> 24:06.916 align:middle
from goal than the other,
then you focus on that one.

24:07.216 --> 24:11.256 align:middle
It actually-it's the time to get
back there times the, you know,

24:11.796 --> 24:13.386 align:middle
times how far it
is from the goal.

24:13.636 --> 24:15.086 align:middle
And that was clearly inflation.

24:15.146 --> 24:17.426 align:middle
So our focus was very
much on inflation.

24:17.876 --> 24:21.236 align:middle
As-and this is what we
referred to in the statement.

24:21.466 --> 24:26.656 align:middle
As inflation has come down,
now to below 3 percent

24:26.656 --> 24:30.166 align:middle
on a-on a 12-month basis,

24:30.166 --> 24:34.326 align:middle
it's become-we're now
focusing [on] the other goal.

24:34.326 --> 24:37.716 align:middle
The employment goal now
comes back into focus.

24:37.796 --> 24:40.426 align:middle
And so we are focusing on it.

24:40.866 --> 24:42.616 align:middle
And, and that's how
we think about that.

24:42.986 --> 24:43.256 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:44.336 --> 24:48.626 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

24:48.806 --> 24:50.666 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

24:50.666 --> 24:52.846 align:middle
Thanks a lot for the opportunity
to ask the questions.

24:52.846 --> 24:56.466 align:middle
Just to go back to the answer
before the previous one,

24:57.376 --> 24:59.776 align:middle
it seemed to suggest that
you think the likeliest path

24:59.776 --> 25:04.106 align:middle
of inflation is one that's going
to allow you to have a situation

25:04.106 --> 25:07.216 align:middle
where rates are lower
at the end of the year

25:07.216 --> 25:08.646 align:middle
than they are right now.

25:08.646 --> 25:10.596 align:middle
It'd be good if you could
just confirm whether

25:10.596 --> 25:12.076 align:middle
or not that's a correct reading.

25:12.706 --> 25:17.636 align:middle
And the Q1 GDP print
has led to some-some

25:17.636 --> 25:20.226 align:middle
to start mentioning
the term "stagflation"

25:20.266 --> 25:22.096 align:middle
with respect to the
U.S. economy.

25:22.096 --> 25:26.916 align:middle
Do you or anyone else on the
FOMC think this is now a risk?

25:28.256 --> 25:28.946 align:middle
Thank you.

25:29.376 --> 25:29.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:30.166 --> 25:32.276 align:middle
Yeah. I'm not dealing
really in likelihoods.

25:32.276 --> 25:35.826 align:middle
I think there are-there are
paths that the economy can take

25:35.826 --> 25:38.486 align:middle
that would involve cuts and
there are paths that wouldn't.

25:39.006 --> 25:42.266 align:middle
And I don't have great
confidence in which

25:42.266 --> 25:45.376 align:middle
of those paths-I think I would
say my personal forecast is

25:45.376 --> 25:47.286 align:middle
that we will begin to
see further progress

25:47.646 --> 25:48.596 align:middle
on inflation this year.

25:49.146 --> 25:51.346 align:middle
I don't know that it will
be enough, sufficient.

25:51.566 --> 25:52.466 align:middle
I don't know that it won't.

25:52.676 --> 25:55.846 align:middle
I think we're going to have to
let the data lead us on that.

25:56.586 --> 25:59.686 align:middle
In terms of your question-your
second question was stagflation.

26:00.566 --> 26:05.436 align:middle
I guess I would say I was around
for stagflation, and it was,

26:05.836 --> 26:08.596 align:middle
you know, 10 percent-10
percent unemployment.

26:09.006 --> 26:11.806 align:middle
It was high single-digits
inflation.

26:12.186 --> 26:18.546 align:middle
Right now we have-and very slow
growth-so right now we have 3

26:18.546 --> 26:20.386 align:middle
percent growth, which
is, you know,

26:21.076 --> 26:23.796 align:middle
pretty solid growth I
would say, by any measure,

26:24.196 --> 26:26.596 align:middle
and we have inflation
running under 3 percent.

26:26.596 --> 26:30.036 align:middle
So I don't-I don't
really understand

26:30.036 --> 26:30.956 align:middle
where that's coming from.

26:31.146 --> 26:35.866 align:middle
And, in addition, I would
say most forecasters,

26:35.866 --> 26:41.796 align:middle
including our forecasting,
was that last year's level

26:41.796 --> 26:44.626 align:middle
of growth was very high-3.4
percent in, I guess,

26:44.656 --> 26:47.366 align:middle
the fourth quarter, you
know-and probably not going

26:47.366 --> 26:49.806 align:middle
to be sustained and
would come down.

26:49.806 --> 26:51.916 align:middle
But that would be-that
would be our forecast.

26:51.916 --> 26:53.206 align:middle
That wouldn't be stagflation.

26:53.206 --> 26:55.646 align:middle
That would still be to a
very healthy level of growth.

26:56.096 --> 26:57.636 align:middle
And, of course, with
inflation, you know,

26:57.636 --> 27:00.566 align:middle
our-we will return
inflation to 2 percent,

27:01.166 --> 27:03.606 align:middle
and that won't be-so
I don't see the "stag"

27:03.606 --> 27:04.656 align:middle
or the "flation," actually.

27:04.936 --> 27:05.406 align:middle
[Laughter] MICHELLE SMITH.

27:05.406 --> 27:07.146 align:middle
Michael McKee.

27:08.276 --> 27:10.116 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

27:10.926 --> 27:13.296 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

27:13.456 --> 27:17.186 align:middle
The Vice Chair of the FOMC
said recently that he's willing

27:17.186 --> 27:20.506 align:middle
to consider the idea that
potential growth has moved up.

27:20.506 --> 27:23.246 align:middle
And, of course, he's "Mr.
Potential Growth/r*."

27:23.406 --> 27:26.786 align:middle
Do you share that view,
and would that imply

27:26.846 --> 27:29.126 align:middle
that maybe policy
isn't tight enough?

27:30.756 --> 27:31.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:31.506 --> 27:36.146 align:middle
So I think I would take that
question this way: We saw a year

27:36.146 --> 27:40.256 align:middle
of very high productivity growth
in 2023, and we saw a year of,

27:40.256 --> 27:43.676 align:middle
I think, negative
productivity growth in 2022.

27:44.176 --> 27:48.726 align:middle
So I think it's hard
to draw from the data.

27:49.136 --> 27:50.376 align:middle
I mean, the question is,

27:50.376 --> 27:52.996 align:middle
will productivity
run-there are two questions.

27:52.996 --> 27:55.926 align:middle
One is: Will productivity
run persistently

27:55.926 --> 27:57.206 align:middle
above its longer-run trend?

27:57.206 --> 27:58.136 align:middle
I don't think we know that.

27:58.516 --> 28:00.146 align:middle
In terms of potential
output, though,

28:00.666 --> 28:02.356 align:middle
that's a separate question.

28:02.396 --> 28:08.196 align:middle
We've had a-what amounts to
a, a significant increase

28:08.196 --> 28:09.456 align:middle
in the potential output

28:09.456 --> 28:11.276 align:middle
of the economy that's
not about productivity.

28:11.276 --> 28:15.406 align:middle
It was about having more labor,
frankly, both through-in 2022,

28:15.406 --> 28:17.826 align:middle
both through participation
and also through immigration.

28:18.336 --> 28:21.526 align:middle
So we're very much-like
other forecasters

28:21.526 --> 28:24.296 align:middle
and economists-getting our
arms around what that means

28:24.296 --> 28:26.586 align:middle
for potential output this year

28:26.586 --> 28:29.406 align:middle
and next year-and last
year for that matter, too.

28:29.406 --> 28:32.676 align:middle
So I think-in that case, I think
you really do have a significant

28:32.676 --> 28:34.196 align:middle
increase in potential output,

28:34.566 --> 28:37.006 align:middle
but you've also got-so
you've got more supply.

28:37.556 --> 28:40.096 align:middle
But those people also
come in, they work,

28:40.096 --> 28:41.416 align:middle
they have jobs, they spend.

28:41.416 --> 28:42.556 align:middle
So you've also got demand.

28:43.086 --> 28:47.436 align:middle
So it-there may be-it may
be that you get more supply

28:47.436 --> 28:49.596 align:middle
than you get demand at the
beginning, but, ultimately,

28:50.036 --> 28:52.586 align:middle
it should be neither
inflationary nor disinflationary

28:52.586 --> 28:54.116 align:middle
over, over a longer period.

28:54.526 --> 28:55.016 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

28:55.016 --> 28:58.226 align:middle
You said earlier
that, at this point,

28:58.346 --> 29:01.126 align:middle
you're not really
considering rate increases.

29:01.566 --> 29:05.946 align:middle
If growth is higher, but you're
not considering rate increases,

29:06.306 --> 29:07.936 align:middle
does that imply that
you're more worried

29:07.936 --> 29:12.336 align:middle
about causing the economy to
slow too much than you are

29:12.336 --> 29:13.856 align:middle
about inflation taking
off again?

29:13.856 --> 29:14.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:14.726 --> 29:19.056 align:middle
No. I think we, we believe our
policy stance is in a good place

29:19.056 --> 29:21.426 align:middle
and is appropriate to
the current situation.

29:22.006 --> 29:25.196 align:middle
We believe it's restrictive,
and, you know, we-our evidence

29:25.196 --> 29:26.566 align:middle
for that, I went over earlier.

29:26.656 --> 29:27.926 align:middle
You see it in the labor market.

29:27.956 --> 29:29.776 align:middle
You see it in
inflation-sensitive spending,

29:30.136 --> 29:32.016 align:middle
where demand has
clearly come down a lot

29:32.176 --> 29:33.336 align:middle
over the past few years.

29:33.806 --> 29:37.386 align:middle
And that's, that's more
from monetary policy,

29:37.386 --> 29:38.636 align:middle
whereas the supply
side of things

29:38.636 --> 29:40.326 align:middle
that are happening are
more on the supply side.

29:40.356 --> 29:42.656 align:middle
So that's how I would
think about it.

29:42.656 --> 29:43.756 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:44.246 --> 29:46.056 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

29:46.216 --> 29:47.756 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

29:47.756 --> 29:49.266 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

29:49.266 --> 29:52.026 align:middle
So GDP growth is
about 2 percent.

29:52.026 --> 29:54.636 align:middle
Inflation-[Un]employment
is about 4 percent.

29:54.636 --> 29:56.226 align:middle
It feels a lot like
a steady state,

29:56.586 --> 29:58.336 align:middle
and we have 3 percent inflation.

29:58.336 --> 30:00.806 align:middle
So if the data remains the
same that you're seeing,

30:00.806 --> 30:03.976 align:middle
and I know you said you don't
see a rate hike, but it stands

30:03.976 --> 30:06.326 align:middle
to reason that you would need
a rate hike to get from 3

30:06.366 --> 30:07.636 align:middle
to 2 percent inflation.

30:07.956 --> 30:09.886 align:middle
So was there any
discussion about a rate hike

30:09.886 --> 30:13.866 align:middle
in today's meeting, and,
you know, are you satisfied

30:13.906 --> 30:16.346 align:middle
with 3 percent inflation
for the rest of the year?

30:16.666 --> 30:17.886 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:18.056 --> 30:20.126 align:middle
Well, of course we're
not satisfied

30:20.126 --> 30:21.146 align:middle
with 3 percent inflation.

30:21.346 --> 30:24.136 align:middle
"Three percent" can't be in
a sentence with "satisfied."

30:24.946 --> 30:28.396 align:middle
[Laughter] So we will return
inflation to 2 percent

30:28.506 --> 30:30.036 align:middle
over time but over time.

30:30.596 --> 30:34.206 align:middle
And we think our policy stance
is, is appropriate to do that.

30:34.316 --> 30:36.256 align:middle
So if we were to conclude

30:36.576 --> 30:39.876 align:middle
that policy is not
sufficiently restrictive

30:39.926 --> 30:42.736 align:middle
to bring inflation
sustainably down to 2 percent,

30:42.866 --> 30:45.536 align:middle
then that would be what
it would take for us

30:45.536 --> 30:46.666 align:middle
to want to increase rates.

30:46.996 --> 30:47.706 align:middle
We don't see that.

30:47.706 --> 30:48.846 align:middle
We don't see evidence for that.

30:49.496 --> 30:50.456 align:middle
So that's where we are.

30:50.456 --> 30:51.086 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

30:51.186 --> 30:52.886 align:middle
Was there discussion
about a rate hike at all?

30:53.746 --> 30:54.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:54.316 --> 30:58.736 align:middle
So the policy focus has been
on-has really been on what

30:58.736 --> 31:05.136 align:middle
to do about, about holding
the current-the current level

31:05.136 --> 31:05.966 align:middle
of restriction.

31:06.076 --> 31:07.966 align:middle
That's really-that's
part of the policy.

31:08.046 --> 31:09.956 align:middle
That's where the policy
discussion was in the meeting.

31:09.956 --> 31:10.346 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

31:10.346 --> 31:11.586 align:middle
I wanted to follow
[up] on the 3 percent.

31:11.716 --> 31:13.996 align:middle
Is there a time frame
of persistent inflation

31:14.356 --> 31:15.606 align:middle
that would trigger a rate hike?

31:16.106 --> 31:16.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:16.446 --> 31:17.456 align:middle
There isn't any rule.

31:17.456 --> 31:18.446 align:middle
You can't look to a rule.

31:18.446 --> 31:20.486 align:middle
You know, these are-these are
going to be judgment calls.

31:20.676 --> 31:24.526 align:middle
You know, clearly restrictive
monetary policy needs more time

31:24.526 --> 31:25.186 align:middle
to do its job.

31:25.496 --> 31:28.686 align:middle
That, that is pretty clear,
based on what we're seeing.

31:29.296 --> 31:31.736 align:middle
How long that will take and how
patient we should be is going

31:31.736 --> 31:35.076 align:middle
to depend on the totality of the
data, how the outlook evolves.

31:36.766 --> 31:39.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:40.216 --> 31:41.226 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

31:41.226 --> 31:42.596 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

31:42.596 --> 31:43.986 align:middle
You've talked about
your commitment

31:43.986 --> 31:45.936 align:middle
to being apolitical
and nonpartisan.

31:45.976 --> 31:48.246 align:middle
And I was just wondering, given
that it's an election year,

31:48.246 --> 31:52.376 align:middle
is the bar for rate changes
higher close to an election?

31:52.946 --> 31:57.346 align:middle
And, similarly, is there a
significant economic difference

31:57.396 --> 32:00.526 align:middle
between, you know,
starting to cut in, say,

32:00.526 --> 32:01.696 align:middle
September versus December?

32:01.696 --> 32:03.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:03.596 --> 32:06.896 align:middle
So we're, we're always going to
do what we think the right thing

32:06.896 --> 32:10.336 align:middle
for the economy is when we
come to that consensus view

32:10.336 --> 32:11.786 align:middle
that it's the right thing
to do for the economy.

32:11.826 --> 32:12.636 align:middle
That's our record.

32:12.756 --> 32:13.416 align:middle
That's what we do.

32:13.936 --> 32:15.956 align:middle
We're not looking
at anything else.

32:15.956 --> 32:18.706 align:middle
You know, it's hard enough to
get the economics right here.

32:18.956 --> 32:22.356 align:middle
These are difficult things,
and if, if we were to take

32:22.356 --> 32:24.576 align:middle
on a whole, another,
set of factors and use

32:24.576 --> 32:27.306 align:middle
that as a new filter, it would
reduce the likelihood we'd

32:27.306 --> 32:28.576 align:middle
actually get the
economics right.

32:29.026 --> 32:30.626 align:middle
So that's how we think
about it around here.

32:30.736 --> 32:32.216 align:middle
And, you know, we're
at peace over it.

32:32.216 --> 32:34.996 align:middle
We know that we'll do what
we think is the right thing,

32:35.496 --> 32:36.606 align:middle
when we think it's
the right thing.

32:36.766 --> 32:37.676 align:middle
And we'll all do that.

32:37.676 --> 32:39.966 align:middle
And that's, that's how
everybody around here thinks.

32:40.386 --> 32:42.326 align:middle
So I can't say it enough,

32:42.826 --> 32:44.936 align:middle
that we just don't-we just
don't go down that road.

32:44.936 --> 32:46.326 align:middle
If you go down that
road, where do you stop?

32:46.996 --> 32:48.576 align:middle
And, and so we're
not on that road.

32:48.576 --> 32:51.076 align:middle
We're on the road where we're
serving all the American people

32:51.596 --> 32:53.766 align:middle
and making our decisions
based on the data

32:54.106 --> 32:55.746 align:middle
and how those data
affect the outlook

32:56.286 --> 32:57.316 align:middle
and the balance of risks.

32:58.336 --> 32:58.896 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

32:58.896 --> 33:01.946 align:middle
And then, is there a significant
difference between, you know,

33:01.946 --> 33:04.996 align:middle
whether you start in, say,
September versus December?

33:04.996 --> 33:05.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:05.486 --> 33:05.966 align:middle
There's- VICTORIA GUIDA.

33:05.966 --> 33:06.656 align:middle
An economic difference.

33:06.656 --> 33:06.866 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:06.866 --> 33:09.266 align:middle
-a significant difference
between an institution

33:09.706 --> 33:11.836 align:middle
that takes into account
all sorts

33:11.836 --> 33:13.996 align:middle
of political events
and one that doesn't.

33:14.626 --> 33:16.836 align:middle
That's where the significant
difference is, and, you know,

33:16.836 --> 33:18.866 align:middle
we're-we just don't do that.

33:18.866 --> 33:20.856 align:middle
I mean, you can go back
and read the transcripts

33:20.856 --> 33:24.726 align:middle
for every-this is
my fourth election,

33:25.086 --> 33:26.416 align:middle
fourth presidential
election here.

33:26.416 --> 33:29.486 align:middle
Read all the transcripts,
and see if anybody mentions,

33:29.776 --> 33:32.686 align:middle
in any way, the pending
election.

33:32.686 --> 33:33.956 align:middle
It just isn't part
of our thinking.

33:33.956 --> 33:35.276 align:middle
It's not what we're hired to do.

33:35.636 --> 33:37.386 align:middle
If we start down
that road, again,

33:37.386 --> 33:38.846 align:middle
I don't know how you stop.

33:39.286 --> 33:40.626 align:middle
So. MICHELLE SMITH.

33:41.706 --> 33:45.596 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

33:45.596 --> 33:46.436 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

33:46.686 --> 33:48.536 align:middle
Question about the labor
market-you've mentioned a few

33:48.536 --> 33:50.436 align:middle
times that the labor
market is normalizing.

33:50.436 --> 33:53.056 align:middle
Certainly, today's JOLTS data
suggested that things are kind

33:53.056 --> 33:54.826 align:middle
of getting back to
pre-pandemic levels.

33:55.366 --> 33:57.456 align:middle
One thing that hasn't
normalized is wage growth,

33:57.456 --> 33:58.666 align:middle
which is still quite
a bit stronger

33:58.666 --> 33:59.596 align:middle
than before the pandemic.

33:59.946 --> 34:01.976 align:middle
I wonder if you can
share your analysis of,

34:02.036 --> 34:03.306 align:middle
of why that's happening.

34:03.306 --> 34:06.296 align:middle
Is it a lagging indicator, or
is something else going on?

34:06.636 --> 34:07.036 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:07.036 --> 34:08.156 align:middle
So I think if you go back

34:08.236 --> 34:11.576 align:middle
to where wages peaked-wage
increases peaked a couple,

34:11.576 --> 34:14.966 align:middle
three years ago-essentially
all wage measures have come

34:14.966 --> 34:16.326 align:middle
down substantially
[when compared] to that.

34:16.826 --> 34:18.876 align:middle
But they are not, not down to

34:18.876 --> 34:21.176 align:middle
where they were before
the pandemic.

34:21.176 --> 34:23.286 align:middle
They're still roughly a
percentage point higher.

34:23.876 --> 34:27.226 align:middle
And we've seen pretty consistent
progress but not uniformly.

34:27.226 --> 34:32.116 align:middle
And you'll note the ECI reading
from Tuesday was-it was expected

34:32.116 --> 34:36.076 align:middle
to be-to have come down, and,
essentially, it was flat year

34:36.076 --> 34:38.756 align:middle
over year, you know,
I think roughly.

34:38.756 --> 34:42.406 align:middle
So yeah, I mean, it's-that
part of it is bumpy.

34:42.906 --> 34:46.876 align:middle
And, again, we don't target
wage increases, but, you know,

34:46.996 --> 34:48.296 align:middle
in the longer run,

34:48.996 --> 34:54.226 align:middle
if you have-if you have wage
increases running higher

34:54.396 --> 34:58.346 align:middle
than productivity would
warrant, then, you know,

34:58.346 --> 35:00.956 align:middle
there will be inflationary
pressures.

35:01.066 --> 35:04.176 align:middle
Employers will raise prices
over time if that's the case.

35:04.176 --> 35:06.346 align:middle
So we've seen progress.

35:06.596 --> 35:09.126 align:middle
It has been inconsistent,

35:09.206 --> 35:11.996 align:middle
but we have seen a
substantial decline overall.

35:12.546 --> 35:14.606 align:middle
But we have a ways
to go on that.

35:15.906 --> 35:17.706 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:18.456 --> 35:19.656 align:middle
Scott. Nancy.

35:19.656 --> 35:20.336 align:middle
I'm sorry.

35:23.176 --> 35:26.116 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

35:27.346 --> 35:27.936 align:middle
Hey, Chair Powell.

35:27.936 --> 35:31.326 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

35:31.326 --> 35:34.746 align:middle
You mentioned consumers, and
consumers are feeling the weight

35:34.746 --> 35:35.996 align:middle
of interest rates right now.

35:36.476 --> 35:38.306 align:middle
Mortgage rates are
up, as are rates

35:38.306 --> 35:39.986 align:middle
for car loans, credit cards.

35:40.496 --> 35:42.596 align:middle
People looking to borrow
are very discouraged.

35:42.596 --> 35:44.696 align:middle
That's reflected in their
views on the economy.

35:45.016 --> 35:45.796 align:middle
What would you say to them?

35:45.796 --> 35:47.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:47.786 --> 35:50.036 align:middle
Well, the thing that
hurts everybody,

35:50.036 --> 35:52.296 align:middle
and particularly people

35:52.336 --> 35:55.306 align:middle
in the lower-income
brackets, is inflation.

35:55.506 --> 35:57.726 align:middle
If you're a person who's
living paycheck to paycheck,

35:58.386 --> 36:03.276 align:middle
and suddenly all the things you
buy-the fundamentals of life-go

36:03.276 --> 36:06.136 align:middle
up in price, you, you are
in trouble right away.

36:06.696 --> 36:09.176 align:middle
And so, with those people
in mind, in particular,

36:09.176 --> 36:11.386 align:middle
what we're doing is,
we're using our tools

36:11.386 --> 36:12.456 align:middle
to bring down inflation.

36:13.036 --> 36:15.066 align:middle
It will take some time,
but we will succeed.

36:15.066 --> 36:17.206 align:middle
And we will bring inflation
back down to 2 percent,

36:17.306 --> 36:19.026 align:middle
and then people won't have
to worry about it again.

36:19.446 --> 36:20.276 align:middle
That's what we're doing,

36:20.276 --> 36:22.326 align:middle
and we know that it's
painful and inconvenient.

36:22.326 --> 36:26.806 align:middle
But the dividends will be
paid and will be very large.

36:26.806 --> 36:28.776 align:middle
And, and everyone will
share in those dividends,

36:29.476 --> 36:30.926 align:middle
and we've made quite
a lot of progress

36:30.926 --> 36:32.346 align:middle
if you can think about it.

36:32.346 --> 36:35.686 align:middle
I think core-I think
headline-core PCE [inflation]

36:35.836 --> 36:39.206 align:middle
peaked at 5.8 [percent].

36:39.206 --> 36:42.126 align:middle
Now it's at-anyway,
headline peaked at 7.1.

36:42.126 --> 36:43.116 align:middle
Now it's at 2.7.

36:43.116 --> 36:44.666 align:middle
Don't want to get that wrong.

36:44.666 --> 36:45.706 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

36:45.706 --> 36:46.426 align:middle
No, you don't.

36:46.426 --> 36:48.866 align:middle
Quick follow-up-are current
interest rates really doing

36:48.866 --> 36:51.196 align:middle
that much, though, to
fight inflation right now

36:51.276 --> 36:52.176 align:middle
for those consumers?

36:52.846 --> 36:53.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:53.196 --> 36:55.006 align:middle
Yes. I mean, I think-I think

36:55.006 --> 36:58.726 align:middle
that restrictive monetary policy
is doing what it's supposed

36:58.726 --> 37:02.016 align:middle
to do, and it's-but it's
also, in this case unusually,

37:02.636 --> 37:06.086 align:middle
working alongside and with the
healing of the supply side.

37:06.086 --> 37:09.956 align:middle
This, this-what was different
this time was that a big part

37:09.956 --> 37:11.366 align:middle
of the source of the inflation

37:11.366 --> 37:14.716 align:middle
and the reason why we're
having this conversation is

37:14.716 --> 37:18.426 align:middle
that we had this supply-side
kind of collapse, with shortages

37:18.426 --> 37:21.046 align:middle
and bottlenecks and
all that kind of thing.

37:21.106 --> 37:24.886 align:middle
And so-and this was to do with
the shutting down and reopening

37:24.886 --> 37:28.946 align:middle
of the economy and other things
that, that really raised demand.

37:29.406 --> 37:30.746 align:middle
So, many factors did that.

37:30.746 --> 37:34.696 align:middle
So I think now you see those
two things working together,

37:35.276 --> 37:38.356 align:middle
the reversal of those supply
and demand distortions

37:38.536 --> 37:40.496 align:middle
from the pandemic and
the response to it,

37:40.666 --> 37:43.086 align:middle
along with restrictive
monetary policy.

37:43.086 --> 37:45.606 align:middle
Those two things are working
to bring down inflation,

37:45.606 --> 37:47.086 align:middle
and we've made a
lot of progress.

37:47.086 --> 37:49.036 align:middle
Let's remember how
far we've come.

37:49.106 --> 37:50.266 align:middle
And we have a ways to go.

37:50.266 --> 37:52.636 align:middle
We've got work left to
do, but we're not looking

37:52.636 --> 37:54.266 align:middle
at the very high inflation rates

37:54.266 --> 37:55.686 align:middle
that we were seeing
two years ago.

37:57.586 --> 37:58.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:58.296 --> 37:58.576 align:middle
Courtenay.

37:59.196 --> 38:00.796 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

38:00.796 --> 38:01.826 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

38:01.876 --> 38:04.396 align:middle
Thanks for taking our
questions, Chair Powell.

38:04.396 --> 38:05.756 align:middle
I wanted to follow

38:05.756 --> 38:09.846 align:middle
up on something you mentioned
earlier on housing inflation.

38:10.036 --> 38:12.596 align:middle
There's kind of been this
long-awaited disinflation

38:12.596 --> 38:14.466 align:middle
in shelter that still
hasn't arrived.

38:14.766 --> 38:19.146 align:middle
So I guess two questions, how do
you explain the substantial lags

38:19.146 --> 38:21.276 align:middle
between some of the
private-sector data we're seeing

38:21.666 --> 38:25.656 align:middle
and the government data,
and how confident are you

38:25.656 --> 38:27.316 align:middle
that rents will be helpful

38:27.316 --> 38:29.236 align:middle
on the inflation front
in the coming months?

38:30.186 --> 38:30.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:30.866 --> 38:35.606 align:middle
So, essentially, there
are-there are a number of places

38:35.606 --> 38:38.756 align:middle
in the economy where there
are just lag structures built

38:38.756 --> 38:42.776 align:middle
into the inflation process,
and housing is one of them.

38:42.776 --> 38:44.906 align:middle
So when you have-when,

38:44.906 --> 38:47.936 align:middle
when someone goes
to-a new person goes

38:47.936 --> 38:50.546 align:middle
to rent an apartment,
that's called market rent,

38:50.826 --> 38:53.026 align:middle
and you can see market rents
are barely going up at all.

38:53.026 --> 38:55.286 align:middle
The inflation in those
has been very low.

38:55.986 --> 39:00.006 align:middle
But it takes-before that,
they were incredibly high.

39:00.006 --> 39:02.936 align:middle
They sort of led
the, the high part.

39:03.396 --> 39:10.226 align:middle
So what happens is, those market
rents take years, actually,

39:10.226 --> 39:14.216 align:middle
to get all the way
into rents for tenants

39:14.216 --> 39:15.996 align:middle
who are rolling over
their leases.

39:16.126 --> 39:19.416 align:middle
Landlords don't tend to
charge as much of an increase

39:19.826 --> 39:22.466 align:middle
to a rollover tenant,
for whatever reason.

39:22.886 --> 39:25.616 align:middle
And what that does is
it builds up a sort

39:25.616 --> 39:27.766 align:middle
of an unrealized
portion of increases

39:27.766 --> 39:29.146 align:middle
when there have been
big increases.

39:29.146 --> 39:31.626 align:middle
And what happens is, you
know-it's complicated,

39:31.626 --> 39:34.106 align:middle
but the story is it just takes
some time for that to get in.

39:34.456 --> 39:37.916 align:middle
Now I am confident that, as
long as market rents remain low,

39:38.026 --> 39:40.786 align:middle
this is going to show up
in measured inflation,

39:41.526 --> 39:43.646 align:middle
assuming that market
rents do remain low.

39:44.196 --> 39:47.126 align:middle
How-what will be the
exact timing of it?

39:47.126 --> 39:49.016 align:middle
I think we've learned
that the lags are longer.

39:49.016 --> 39:51.756 align:middle
We now think significantly
longer than we thought

39:51.756 --> 39:54.886 align:middle
at the beginning, and so
confident that it will come

39:54.886 --> 39:56.776 align:middle
but not so confident
in the timing of it.

39:57.316 --> 39:59.326 align:middle
But, yes, I expect that,
that this will happen.

39:59.326 --> 39:59.896 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

40:00.546 --> 40:01.216 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

40:01.716 --> 40:02.446 align:middle
Nick Jasinski.

40:02.846 --> 40:04.696 align:middle
NICHOLAS JASINSKI.

40:04.876 --> 40:06.346 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell,
for taking the questions.

40:06.346 --> 40:08.616 align:middle
This is Nicholas Jasinski
from Barron's Magazine.

40:09.146 --> 40:12.396 align:middle
It seems that over the past
three or four years, economies

40:12.396 --> 40:14.226 align:middle
and central banks in
developed markets, at least,

40:14.226 --> 40:16.586 align:middle
have been on more or
less the same trajectory:

40:17.476 --> 40:19.636 align:middle
easing during the
pandemic, fighting inflation

40:19.636 --> 40:21.716 align:middle
with restrictive
policy on the way out.

40:21.716 --> 40:24.716 align:middle
It feels like that may be
ending in 2024 based on some

40:24.716 --> 40:26.926 align:middle
of the economic data from
Europe and the U.S. and Japan

40:26.926 --> 40:28.586 align:middle
and statements from those
central banks as well.

40:28.586 --> 40:30.986 align:middle
So my question is, what,
what considerations

40:30.986 --> 40:34.686 align:middle
or risks does a period of
more divergent global economic

40:34.686 --> 40:38.056 align:middle
trajectories and central bank
policies present for the FOMC?

40:38.726 --> 40:39.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:39.956 --> 40:41.356 align:middle
So that-you're right.

40:41.356 --> 40:43.226 align:middle
I think that, that
may happen, and,

40:43.276 --> 40:46.426 align:middle
you know that we all serve
domestic mandates, right?

40:46.886 --> 40:49.216 align:middle
So I think the difference
between the United States

40:49.216 --> 40:53.066 align:middle
and other countries that are
now considering rate cuts is

40:53.066 --> 40:56.246 align:middle
that they're just not having
the kind of growth we're having.

40:56.246 --> 40:59.896 align:middle
They have-their inflation is
performing about like ours

40:59.896 --> 41:00.846 align:middle
or maybe a little better,

41:00.916 --> 41:02.556 align:middle
but they're not experiencing
the kind

41:02.556 --> 41:03.606 align:middle
of growth we're experiencing.

41:03.606 --> 41:07.186 align:middle
So we actually have the
luxury of having strong growth

41:07.186 --> 41:09.366 align:middle
and a strong labor market,
very low unemployment,

41:09.366 --> 41:12.616 align:middle
high job creation, and all of
that, and we can be patient.

41:12.786 --> 41:15.426 align:middle
And we will-we'll be
careful and cautious

41:16.026 --> 41:17.996 align:middle
as we approach the
decision to cut rates,

41:17.996 --> 41:20.466 align:middle
whereas I think other
jurisdictions may go

41:20.466 --> 41:20.836 align:middle
before that.

41:21.106 --> 41:23.206 align:middle
In terms of the implications,
you know,

41:23.206 --> 41:27.736 align:middle
I think obviously
markets see it coming.

41:27.736 --> 41:28.856 align:middle
It's priced in now.

41:28.916 --> 41:33.756 align:middle
And so I think the markets
and economies can adapt to it.

41:34.056 --> 41:37.656 align:middle
And I think, you know, we
haven't seen-in addition,

41:37.656 --> 41:38.896 align:middle
for the emerging
market economies,

41:38.896 --> 41:41.256 align:middle
we haven't seen the
kind of turmoil

41:41.256 --> 41:44.316 align:middle
that was more frequent 20
years ago, 30 years ago.

41:44.656 --> 41:45.346 align:middle
And that's, I think,

41:45.346 --> 41:47.086 align:middle
partly because emerging
market countries,

41:47.086 --> 41:49.646 align:middle
many of them have much better
monetary policy frameworks,

41:49.646 --> 41:51.936 align:middle
much more credibility
on inflation.

41:51.936 --> 41:54.636 align:middle
And so they're navigating
this pretty well this time.

41:56.886 --> 41:57.706 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:57.766 --> 42:00.846 align:middle
Jennifer. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

42:01.206 --> 42:02.286 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

42:02.286 --> 42:04.506 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

42:04.506 --> 42:06.546 align:middle
You sort of backed
away from the notion

42:06.546 --> 42:09.176 align:middle
that the economy would
need to encounter pain

42:09.366 --> 42:11.616 align:middle
for inflation to come back down.

42:12.086 --> 42:15.026 align:middle
But, given the sticky inflation
data in the first quarter,

42:15.026 --> 42:19.386 align:middle
can disinflation still happen
along a relatively painless path

42:19.386 --> 42:23.906 align:middle
for the economy, or is some
softening in the labor market

42:23.906 --> 42:26.886 align:middle
and the economy likely needed
to bring inflation back down?

42:27.396 --> 42:27.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:27.666 --> 42:28.416 align:middle
So you're right.

42:28.416 --> 42:32.166 align:middle
I think we thought, and most
people thought, there would have

42:32.166 --> 42:38.106 align:middle
to be probably significant
dislocations somewhere

42:38.106 --> 42:39.666 align:middle
in the economy, perhaps
the labor market,

42:39.736 --> 42:41.056 align:middle
to get inflation all the way

42:41.056 --> 42:42.526 align:middle
down from the very
high levels it was

42:42.526 --> 42:43.696 align:middle
at at the beginning
of this episode.

42:43.966 --> 42:44.726 align:middle
That didn't happen.

42:44.786 --> 42:46.046 align:middle
That's a tremendous result.

42:46.046 --> 42:48.366 align:middle
We're very, of course,
gratified and pleased

42:48.366 --> 42:49.156 align:middle
that that hasn't happened.

42:49.576 --> 42:52.276 align:middle
And, if you look at the dynamics
that enabled that, it really was

42:52.406 --> 42:57.536 align:middle
that this, the-that so much of
the gain was from the unwinding

42:57.606 --> 42:59.976 align:middle
of things that weren't to
do with monetary policy

42:59.976 --> 43:02.316 align:middle
but the unwinding of the
distortions to the economy,

43:03.096 --> 43:05.306 align:middle
you know, supply
problems-supply-side problems

43:05.306 --> 43:08.146 align:middle
and also some, some
demand issues as well.

43:09.006 --> 43:11.396 align:middle
The unwinding of those really
helped inflation come down.

43:11.546 --> 43:14.546 align:middle
Now, as I've said, I'm
not giving up on that.

43:14.546 --> 43:16.776 align:middle
So I think-I think
it is possible that,

43:17.086 --> 43:19.126 align:middle
that those forces
will still work

43:19.126 --> 43:20.686 align:middle
to help us bring inflation down.

43:21.956 --> 43:24.276 align:middle
We can't-we can't be guaranteed
that that's true, though.

43:24.466 --> 43:27.756 align:middle
And so we're-you know, we're
trying to use our tools in a way

43:27.756 --> 43:31.236 align:middle
that keeps the labor market
strong, and the economy strong,

43:31.276 --> 43:33.506 align:middle
but also helps bring
inflation back

43:33.506 --> 43:34.906 align:middle
down to 2 percent sustainably.

43:35.076 --> 43:37.266 align:middle
We will bring inflation down
to 2 percent sustainably.

43:37.266 --> 43:39.766 align:middle
We hope we can do it
without, you know,

43:39.766 --> 43:42.556 align:middle
without significant dislocations

43:42.556 --> 43:43.776 align:middle
in the labor market
or elsewhere.

43:43.776 --> 43:44.606 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

43:44.606 --> 43:47.096 align:middle
And speaking of dislocations
in the labor market:

43:47.266 --> 43:49.406 align:middle
In terms of cutting, you
said, if there were weakness

43:49.406 --> 43:51.776 align:middle
in the job market, that could
be a reason to cut rates.

43:52.116 --> 43:55.056 align:middle
So if the unemployment rate
were to tick above 4 percent

43:55.136 --> 43:58.326 align:middle
but inflation not back down
to your 2 percent target,

43:58.726 --> 44:00.006 align:middle
how would you look at that?

44:00.006 --> 44:02.346 align:middle
Would the unemployment
rate popping back

44:02.346 --> 44:04.046 align:middle
above 4 percent catch
your attention?

44:04.046 --> 44:04.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:04.486 --> 44:08.266 align:middle
You know, I said "an unexpected
weakening" is what I-the way I

44:08.266 --> 44:08.956 align:middle
characterized it.

44:08.956 --> 44:10.776 align:middle
So, you know-and
I'm not going to try

44:10.776 --> 44:12.366 align:middle
to define exactly
what I mean by that.

44:12.426 --> 44:15.116 align:middle
But, you know, it would be-it
would have to be meaningful

44:15.116 --> 44:17.846 align:middle
and get our attention
and lead us to think

44:17.846 --> 44:21.006 align:middle
that the labor market was really
significantly weakening for us

44:21.006 --> 44:21.926 align:middle
to want to react to it.

44:21.926 --> 44:23.126 align:middle
A couple of tenths

44:23.126 --> 44:26.116 align:middle
in the unemployment rate would
be-would probably not do that.

44:26.206 --> 44:27.956 align:middle
But a broader-it would
be a broader thing

44:27.956 --> 44:29.286 align:middle
that would-that would suggest

44:30.036 --> 44:33.356 align:middle
that it would be appropriate
to consider cutting.

44:33.706 --> 44:37.486 align:middle
I think whether you decide to
cut will depend on all the facts

44:37.486 --> 44:39.066 align:middle
and circumstances,
not just that one.

44:39.166 --> 44:40.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:40.836 --> 44:41.496 align:middle
Kyle. KYLE CAMPBELL.

44:43.806 --> 44:45.476 align:middle
Chair Powell, thanks
for taking the question.

44:45.646 --> 44:47.246 align:middle
Kyle Campbell with
American Banker.

44:48.206 --> 44:50.736 align:middle
You've said that broad and
material changes are needed

44:50.736 --> 44:54.426 align:middle
for the Basel III Endgame
proposal, and you've mentioned

44:54.426 --> 44:57.356 align:middle
that a re-proposal is
something that's on the table.

44:57.666 --> 44:59.626 align:middle
As you've had more
time to sort of sit

44:59.626 --> 45:02.036 align:middle
with the public commentary,
process that,

45:02.036 --> 45:04.176 align:middle
and understand the
options available to you,

45:04.176 --> 45:05.286 align:middle
do you have a better sense

45:05.286 --> 45:07.256 align:middle
of whether a re-proposal
will be necessary,

45:07.636 --> 45:10.336 align:middle
and do you have a timeline
in mind for when, you know,

45:10.336 --> 45:12.956 align:middle
some sort of movement
will be made

45:12.956 --> 45:14.866 align:middle
on that front-either
a re-proposal

45:14.866 --> 45:16.236 align:middle
or a move to finalize?

45:16.616 --> 45:17.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:17.656 --> 45:21.206 align:middle
So let me-let me start by saying
that the Fed is committed to,

45:21.836 --> 45:24.986 align:middle
you know, completing
this process and carrying

45:25.906 --> 45:29.126 align:middle
out Basel III Endgame in a
way that's faithful to Basel

45:29.126 --> 45:32.406 align:middle
and also comparable to what
the other large comparable

45:32.406 --> 45:34.196 align:middle
jurisdictions are doing.

45:34.366 --> 45:36.356 align:middle
We haven't made any
decisions on,

45:36.446 --> 45:39.786 align:middle
on policy or on process at all.

45:39.886 --> 45:40.976 align:middle
Nothing. Nothing.

45:40.976 --> 45:42.036 align:middle
No decisions have been made.

45:42.396 --> 45:44.856 align:middle
I'll say again, though,
if we conclude

45:45.096 --> 45:46.546 align:middle
that re-proposal is appropriate,

45:46.546 --> 45:48.456 align:middle
we won't hesitate
to insist on that.

45:48.816 --> 45:49.256 align:middle
KYLE CAMPBELL.

45:49.256 --> 45:53.876 align:middle
And then do you need to resolve
issues with the capital proposal

45:53.876 --> 45:56.476 align:middle
in order to advance other
parts of the regulatory agenda,

45:56.476 --> 45:58.526 align:middle
or do you expect to
continue to make progress

45:58.526 --> 46:00.876 align:middle
on those other agenda items?

46:00.876 --> 46:01.316 align:middle
Thank you.

46:01.516 --> 46:01.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:01.816 --> 46:04.406 align:middle
You know, there's no
mechanical rule in place there.

46:04.406 --> 46:09.076 align:middle
But I would say that the, you
know, the Basel III process is

46:09.136 --> 46:11.716 align:middle
by far the most important
thing and really is, I think,

46:11.716 --> 46:15.086 align:middle
occupying us at this
time in terms of what's,

46:15.226 --> 46:16.336 align:middle
what's-what we're
moving ahead with.

46:16.976 --> 46:17.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:18.256 --> 46:20.656 align:middle
Let's go to Mark for
the last question.

46:21.676 --> 46:22.256 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

46:23.546 --> 46:23.816 align:middle
Thank you.

46:23.816 --> 46:25.596 align:middle
Mark Hamrick with Bankrate.

46:25.596 --> 46:28.626 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, what can you
tell us about the approach

46:28.626 --> 46:31.336 align:middle
that you take with your
role in the sense of trying

46:31.336 --> 46:34.756 align:middle
to achieve consensus, which
you recently identified

46:34.756 --> 46:38.106 align:middle
as a priority, while
allowing for a range

46:38.106 --> 46:39.496 align:middle
of views or even dissent?

46:40.266 --> 46:42.456 align:middle
We don't see many
dissenting votes

46:42.456 --> 46:43.696 align:middle
in the official statements,

46:43.696 --> 46:46.436 align:middle
even when more spirited
discussions are noted

46:46.436 --> 46:47.826 align:middle
in the minutes after the fact.

46:47.826 --> 46:50.126 align:middle
How do you avoid groupthink

46:50.296 --> 46:53.926 align:middle
and avoid a higher risk
of a policy mistake?

46:54.016 --> 46:54.346 align:middle
Thank you.

46:54.346 --> 46:54.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:54.956 --> 46:57.226 align:middle
So I think if you listen
to, and you all do,

46:57.406 --> 47:00.856 align:middle
listen to my 18 colleagues
on the FOMC,

47:00.926 --> 47:03.616 align:middle
you'll see that we do
not lack for a diversity

47:03.616 --> 47:05.506 align:middle
of voices and perspectives.

47:05.506 --> 47:06.126 align:middle
We really don't.

47:06.686 --> 47:08.646 align:middle
And it's one of the
great aspects

47:08.646 --> 47:10.126 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve System.

47:10.126 --> 47:12.586 align:middle
We have 12 Reserve
Banks around the country

47:12.586 --> 47:15.906 align:middle
where they have their own
economic staff, not the people

47:15.906 --> 47:16.696 align:middle
who work here at the Board.

47:16.696 --> 47:17.666 align:middle
There are different people.

47:17.856 --> 47:22.716 align:middle
You know, and so each, each
Reserve Bank has its own culture

47:22.716 --> 47:24.666 align:middle
around monetary policy
and its own approach

47:24.666 --> 47:25.636 align:middle
and that kind of thing.

47:25.636 --> 47:28.466 align:middle
It guarantees you a
diversity of perspectives.

47:28.466 --> 47:31.516 align:middle
So I think that the
perspectives are very diverse.

47:31.966 --> 47:34.706 align:middle
But-and, in terms of-in
terms of dissents,

47:35.236 --> 47:38.536 align:middle
you know, we have dissents.

47:39.586 --> 47:43.526 align:middle
And, you know, a thoughtful
dissent is a good thing,

47:43.526 --> 47:46.586 align:middle
if someone really makes you
think, that kind of thing.

47:46.656 --> 47:50.196 align:middle
But all I can say from my
standpoint is I try-I listen

47:50.196 --> 47:51.166 align:middle
carefully to people.

47:51.166 --> 47:54.966 align:middle
I try to incorporate their
thinking or do everything I can

47:54.966 --> 47:57.636 align:middle
to incorporate their thinking
into what we're doing.

47:58.326 --> 48:01.776 align:middle
And I think many people, if they
feel that's happening, you know,

48:01.776 --> 48:04.296 align:middle
that for most people most of
the time, that'll be enough.

48:04.296 --> 48:09.946 align:middle
And-but I'm not-I mean, it's,
it's not, you know, frowned upon

48:09.946 --> 48:12.906 align:middle
or illegal or against the
rules or anything like that.

48:13.456 --> 48:15.426 align:middle
It just is the way
things come out.

48:15.496 --> 48:18.146 align:middle
And I mean, I think we
have a very diverse group

48:18.146 --> 48:20.356 align:middle
of rounded people-frankly,
more diverse than it used to be

48:20.356 --> 48:24.746 align:middle
in many dimensions, more
diverse from the obvious gender

48:24.746 --> 48:29.026 align:middle
and demographic ways, but
also we have more people

48:29.026 --> 48:30.766 align:middle
who are not Ph.D. economists.

48:31.226 --> 48:34.056 align:middle
So we have people
from business and law

48:34.196 --> 48:36.446 align:middle
and academia and
things like that.

48:36.446 --> 48:38.476 align:middle
So I think we actually
do have quite a good

48:38.476 --> 48:39.586 align:middle
diverse perspective.

48:39.586 --> 48:42.616 align:middle
I think all of us read
these stories about the lack

48:42.616 --> 48:43.906 align:middle
of diversity, and we
look around the room

48:43.906 --> 48:45.046 align:middle
and say, "I don't understand.

48:45.046 --> 48:46.436 align:middle
I really don't understand
what they're talking about."

48:46.536 --> 48:50.026 align:middle
So-but I get the
question, though.

48:51.046 --> 48:55.946 align:middle
Thank you very much.

